A||||3||Dslice||32 EGR CODE||||4||
Z||000000||Dslice||05-20-2001||08:00 PM||||I've got a problem. I get a code 32 when Im driving my 86GT on the freeway. It doesnt happen during street driving. Im pretty sure the egr valve is ok. How do I check if the solenoid is bad. Anyother suggestions.<P>Rick<BR>86 GT||||reg||4
Z||000001||jasonlk||05-20-2001||11:08 PM||||I feel your pain! I had the same problem.  I took it to 3 different mechanics and they did'nt do anything but two of them said they couldn't find anything wrong and another one replaced the oxygen sensor, which was replaced 2 months before by me!  Anyway, I finally tried to fix it myself and I replaced everthing including the EGR Valve, EGR Solenoid, the EGR tubing, and still I had the problem.  Finally, I got sick and tired and decided to take of the plenum, and instantly I noticed that the PCV valve tube was disconnected!  THAT WAS ALL!  I was PO'ED<BR>after I found that and the mechanics didn't even see it!  It did stop the engine light from coming on and reading a code 32, but my problem was getting it to pass emissions, which required me to replace the catalytic converter, which was hollowed out.  Anyway, I hope this helps.||||reg||3
Z||000002||Fformula88||05-21-2001||07:37 AM||||The first thing you want to do is check for Vacumn leaks.  Unfortunately, they are extremely difficult to find at times.  Look over all of the plastic vacumn lines for cracks or breaks, since they get very brittle with age.  Also, the EGR tube may ba cracked.  Also, be sure to look at the ends of the vacumn tubes where the elbows and connections are.  I had one split where it goes into the underside of the throttle body.<P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tom88gt||05-21-2001||10:09 AM||||Vacumn, vacumn, vacumn.  Check this first.  It will save lots of headaches. ||||reg||4
